{"step_index":191,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-07T06:20:48Z","input_tokens":2087,"cache_read_tokens":185454,"output_tokens":801,"thinking":"Windows reserves UDP port 53 for its DNS client cache, which is the root cause of the conflict. However, TCP/UDP ports 8080, 8443, 9090, and custom DNS ports such as 5354, 8053, and 5300 are available and can address the need. Focusing on these alternative ports ensures comprehensive capture of all web browsing on the laptop.\n\n\nThe standard approach for capturing all web traffic involves setting up a local web traffic interceptor and forwarding proxy on the system. By configuring this proxy as the Windows System Proxy, all traffic from browsers and applications will automatically route through it. Implementing a DNS proxy or sinkhole can further enhance control by blocking ad domains and returning a null response.\n\nThe web traffic interceptor analyzes HTTP requests and HTTPS CONNECT requests, specifically examining the target domain extracted from SNI or the Host header. This enables checking domains against a blacklist to identify and block ad or tracker requests, returning a \"Blocked\" response while allowing clean requests to proceed. Each request is logged with a timestamp and the browsed domain.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"github \\\"StevenBlack/hosts\\\" ad blocker python proxy \\\"CONNECT\\\"\""}}]}
{"step_index":192,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-07T06:20:57Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-07T07:20:57+01:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-07T07:21:04+01:00\nThe search for \"github \"StevenBlack/hosts\" ad blocker python proxy \"CONNECT\"\" returned the following summary:\nIf you are looking to build or implement a **Python-based HTTP/HTTPS proxy server** that acts as an ad blocker by leveraging the well-known blocklists from **`StevenBlack/hosts`**[1][2] (which map unwanted tracking, malware, and ad domains to `0.0.0.0` or `127.0.0.1`)[3][4], handling the **`CONNECT`** method is a crucial requirement[5].\n\nUnlike plain HTTP requests (where the proxy sees the full URL like `http://example.com/ad.js`), HTTPS traffic requires an HTTP **`CONNECT`** tunnel[6][5]. When a client wants to load a secure site, it first sends a `CONNECT` command to the proxy (e.g., `CONNECT ad.doubleclick.net:443 HTTP/1.1`), asking the proxy to open a TCP tunnel to the destination.\n\nBelow is an overview of how a Python proxy processes `CONNECT` requests alongside a `StevenBlack`-style hosts blocklist.\n\n---\n\n### Conceptual Architecture of a Hosts-Based Ad-Blocking Proxy\n\n1. **Load the Blocklist:** Download or parse the raw text file from `StevenBlack/hosts` (e.g., the unified hosts file)[7][8] into a fast Python lookup structure (like a `set` for $O(1)$ complexity).\n2. **Start a TCP Proxy Server:** Listen on a local port (e.g., `127.0.0.1:8080`)[9].\n3. **Intercept Client Requests:** \n   * **For standard HTTP:** Read the request line, extract the hostname, and check it against the blocklist.\n   * **For HTTPS (`CONNECT`):** Parse the target host and port out of the `CONNECT` header. Check the hostname against the blocklist *before* opening any tunnel.\n4. **Action:**\n   * **If Blocked:** Drop the connection, return an HTTP `403 Forbidden` (or a `502/504` error code), or reply with a connection close so ads/trackers fail to load silently.\n   * **If Allowed:** Establish a socket connection to the real destination and relay the TCP stream transparently.\n\n---\n\n### Minimal Skeleton: Python Proxy Handling `CONNECT` & Domain Filtering\n\n<truncated 5279 bytes>\n\n[4] [jamieweb.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQETSkFQXQ9l5wgKZpUF0MqtxfC-CBbQir4qmec4hTvSO09dCVCyfYaTMTGBCnAplaCZOFUOz_oHAlhb5-T9fNPsalD7kMld2lu7KX2wF8Cqu-0GR4bqJSVL8gwUbJ3s5TldoXmAlFd_Fg==)\n[5] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH9O7e2F1PWaKMtsZyMFxPaaW7x4PovYU14n-92Fx3yIs1rigurODI0-vjdcayhSpmeNjwAn4lmQ77opnVAi4IMeO4EEI6HPZxDDbf6CEYPjgbr3wlxh2Uy3L8ANrVSA6pNIGjIIr8JoQJUZTKyVf8ixRj2g3ePQQg4MoDraSMx0Dt29xGwD6VxCGmE)\n[6] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGStNA7PZNzxql8-WFbxZlryWj5_hx-EMtfUwfCDF94Z4bxlAYFCg_-uF8PQXXASOLC7YU5SnsXXlZ8RLAQo3SYvT-pJTw6Wt1FzzMbWpvq0GhR5z8BdBn7n4cE4VygPDxa)\n[7] [context7.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFlojMlGGBAJUAYzZcUGbFQ7yOC9nBitkAyfbzB9YkELZ8zNftabibip8avRJvWWE91ORUOAqpnjxBEsylkZGVNVwcn0UqUI4RVqc9dwAXiVEecZGfcYgHDPC8q)\n[8] [norton.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGlId0Xsd-V77hbnNZ-W4jV9y7F2OVqKEVM3ikjOxpv4_ithGmI3mOZzZCWxfSl-ecdTC72elXXv5hakLKSSZMTNkdezeIpFa8A-MjosEkEBY_mh15RJ2vCjCvBvWzKjtT66kfxD_eZCSQheqdS-gE=)\n[9] [codelivly.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHFMKsKFcos5Wopz2-fDsWka_56O2LsiOGxaJCX8rztfpXi4Wv9o2qpY0SUlw0cblmmFS9ZHg_o30udNDI4QicmOAQanE73VbBAz6NQki3e-URCxBUiy48KK8vccfxaUm14L6fVeW26lOrupxJNiwakstQZdsvTxloN1QuBQQ==)\n[10] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF5JDzcdt9IPL1nehGr5ww96lmwNV_DX221IcS7rnEwGHfwCe_xqRMq0DLBXwxhay7wMNsH-UypdZOCT4FSb8TgWCV9D0yFqKgIPQP6optC1zLRdvpaOYfJjwy_63I4JpjRmAwUJ3p-3qkQdRkIxy6pHwv0Mux1nz4j1yGElWYL4sr40fKrO838iKoY0qH7HPgFITQ=)\n[11] [github.com](https://vertexais